\begin{abstract}
This progress report investigates hybrid quantum-classical methods for multi-label emotion classification in Bengali, a low-resource language with limited labelled data and standardized benchmarks. The study combines pretrained transformer representations with variational quantum circuits and examines how quantum embedding, data encoding, entanglement, circuit depth, measurement, and optimization affect the separability of emotion representations. Two custom four-qubit architectures are explored to improve expressive capacity under limited quantum resources. The broader objective is to develop trainable quantum classifiers that improve recognition of overlapping and minority emotion classes while remaining competitive with conventional baseline models.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
This progress report summarizes the investigation of variational quantum circuits (VQCs) and quantum embedding strategies for emotion classification, with particular emphasis on Bengali as a low-resource language. The work completed so far indicates that the geometry and separability of the learned representation space depend strongly on the selected quantum embedding. This observation motivates a systematic study of custom VQC architectures, alternative data-encoding schemes, entanglement patterns, circuit depth, measurement strategies, and optimizer variants. The broader objective is to improve multi-class emotion recognition and exceed conventional baseline performance in the approximate range of 50-60\%. The report documents the present conclusions, discusses the limitations of the current approach, and defines a structured programme for developing more expressive and trainable quantum classifiers.

Emotion classification from text is an important natural language processing task with applications in sentiment-aware dialogue systems, social-media analysis, public-opinion monitoring, and human-computer interaction. The problem becomes substantially more difficult for low-resource languages such as Bengali because labelled corpora, language-specific pretrained models, balanced emotion datasets, and standardized benchmarks are comparatively limited. The difficulty increases further when the objective is multi-class emotion recognition rather than binary sentiment classification. Closely related emotions may occupy overlapping regions of the representation space, and minority classes can be suppressed by dominant labels.

The present work studies whether hybrid quantum-classical models can provide a useful representation and decision mechanism for this setting.

\section{Literature Review and Related Work}
Recent advances in low-resource natural language processing (NLP) demonstrate that pretrained language models such as BanglaBERT can provide strong contextual text representations \cite{bhattacharjee2022banglabert}; however, multi-label emotion classification remains constrained by rigid output thresholds and can suffer substantial performance degradation under strict evaluation metrics such as the Exact Match Ratio (EMR). To address representation bottlenecks, near-term quantum machine learning (QML) paradigms use parameterized quantum circuits (PQCs) to map classical feature vectors into quantum Hilbert spaces $\mathcal{H}$ \cite{chang2025primer}. To increase expressivity in low-qubit architectures, Pérez-Salinas et al. introduced the data re-uploading paradigm, in which classical feature-encoding layers $U(x)$ are interleaved with trainable unitary transformations $U(\theta)$ across $L$ layers \cite{perez2020data}:
\begin{equation}
U(\theta, x) = \prod_{l=1}^{L} U(\theta_l) U(x) .
\end{equation}
Repeatedly injecting the input allows constrained circuits to represent more complex decision functions than a single encoding layer. Building on these theoretical foundations, the present work combines a BanglaBERT feature extractor with a data-re-uploading VQC ansatz and per-label decision-threshold calibration. This design targets class imbalance and seeks to improve joint subset-prediction accuracy in multi-label Bengali emotion classification.

\section{Dataset}
This study uses the EmoNoBa dataset, a manually annotated collection of 22,698 public Bangla comments gathered from social media platforms \cite{islam2022emonoba}. The comments span 12 domains, including personal matters, politics, and health, and are labeled with six fine-grained emotion categories based on the Junto Emotion Wheel. The dataset preserves the informal and noisy characteristics of online Bangla text, making it suitable for evaluating emotion-classification models under realistic conditions.

\section{Methodology}

\subsection{Variational Quantum Circuits}
Following the transformer encoding, the 768-dimensional CLS embedding is projected by a trainable linear layer to a low-dimensional feature vector. Depending on the variant, the vector is squashed by $\tanh$ and fed directly into a four-qubit variational quantum circuit (VQC); the circuit outputs are mapped back through a post-quantum linear layer and a ReLU activation before the final classifier. Two custom VQCs are investigated.

\paragraph{Custom 1 re-uploading VQC.} The circuit acts on $n=4$ qubits and consumes an $\mathbb{R}^{8}$ vector, i.e. two features per qubit. 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] \textbf{Encoding.} For each qubit $q$, its feature pair $(x_{2q}, x_{2q+1})$ is first rotated by a learnable phase $\phi_q$:
    \begin{equation}
    x_{2q}^{\prime} = s_q (\cos \phi_q x_{2q} - \sin \phi_q x_{2q+1}) ,
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}
    x_{2q+1}^{\prime} = s_q (\sin \phi_q x_{2q} + \cos \phi_q x_{2q+1}) ,
    \end{equation}
    where $s_q$ is a second learnable amplitude per qubit. The rotated values are applied as rotation angles $R_X(x_{2q}^{\prime})$ and $R_Y(x_{2q+1}^{\prime})$ on qubit $q$.
    \item[(ii)] \textbf{Ansatz.} Each variational block applies a general single-qubit rotation $R_Z R_Y R_Z \equiv \text{Rot}$ on every qubit, followed by a ring of controlled-$Z$ entangling gates $CZ[q, (q+1) \bmod 4]$.
    \item[(iii)] \textbf{Data re-uploading.} The data-dependent encoding is inserted before both variational blocks, i.e. encode $\rightarrow$ ansatz $\rightarrow$ encode $\rightarrow$ ansatz, so the input is re-supplied between trainable layers.
    \item[(iv)] \textbf{Measurement.} Both $\langle Z_q \rangle$ and $\langle X_q \rangle$ are measured on all four qubits, yielding $2n=8$ expectation values that feed the post-quantum layer. The circuit contains 20 trainable parameters: 4 phase parameters, 4 scale parameters, and 12 ansatz angles.
\end{enumerate}

